Ladybird draws scaled images with one antialiased destination edge,
without multiplying it by a second clip against the same rectangle.
The clips established by paint callers and the source sampling bounds
still apply. The isolated-backdrop path also draws its rectangle with
one edge coverage, while its shader supplies the blended image color.

Blink uses BitmapImage::Draw to pass the source and destination bounds
to PaintCanvas::drawImageRect, without a second destination-edge clip.
WebKit does the same in GraphicsContextSkia::drawNativeImage, including
its image draw for a shadow. In Gecko’s Skia path, DrawTargetSkia::
DrawSurface calls drawImageRect through AutoPaintSetup, which sets
antialiasing without adding another destination-rectangle clip.

Chrome renders the reported four-image reduction without light seams.
The reduction and a single-image reference differ by at most one per
color channel at three intersection pixels. The regression test allows
only that measured rounding difference.

The perspective-image screenshot also reflects the single edge
coverage. Across its 711 changed pixels, the mean channel error
against Chrome falls from 33.8 to 6.5 after removing the duplicate clip.
